{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163498",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14164351/assets/771929267e75b5d45b9777399c784fafc570386834eee6738fead9ff1bb78e5e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(0)^{4/3}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(8)^{4/3}+2 \\\\ 32 & =12k+2 \\\\ 12k & =30 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
